WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.009
Day 2 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.559 --> 00:00:07.824
How the SDET role is being reshaped by AI.

00:00:08.374 --> 00:00:13.296
What changes and what doesn't in the SDET role, and how to build your skills matrix

00:00:13.296 --> 00:00:14.541
for the next 30 days.

00:00:15.841 --> 00:00:20.823
The most repeated question in the testing community is whether AI will replace the

00:00:20.823 --> 00:00:21.127
SDET.

00:00:21.427 --> 00:00:25.319
It's the wrong question: the work doesn't disappear, it moves.

00:00:25.619 --> 00:00:30.370
Writing a test stopped being the scarce part, because an agent does it in seconds.

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:35.177
What's scarce now is knowing what is worth testing, giving context to whoever writes

00:00:35.177 --> 00:00:38.288
it and being able to state that the result is trustworthy.

00:00:39.588 --> 00:00:43.870
This guide describes that shift with four new competencies and three that don't

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:47.881
change, and ends with a matrix to measure yourself without self-deception.

00:00:48.181 --> 00:00:52.982
It isn't a list of trendy tools: it's a skills map with verifiable evidence for each

00:00:52.982 --> 00:00:53.210
one.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.208
To follow this guide you need:

00:00:56.758 --> 00:00:58.862
A spreadsheet or a notes page.

00:00:59.412 --> 00:01:01.319
Your current job description.

00:01:01.869 --> 00:01:03.450
30 honest minutes.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:05.786
Step 1.

00:01:06.086 --> 00:01:08.167
Before and now, in writing.

00:01:08.717 --> 00:01:12.122
Write what you did two years ago and what you do today.

00:01:12.422 --> 00:01:16.743
The core shift: from writing every test to designing, guiding, reviewing and

00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:18.392
validating systems that test.

00:01:18.942 --> 00:01:22.021
Take your job description and your last ten tasks.

00:01:22.321 --> 00:01:27.347
For each one, ask which part was producing (writing test code, data, reports) and

00:01:27.347 --> 00:01:32.063
which part was deciding (what to cover, what risk to accept, which result to

00:01:32.063 --> 00:01:32.622
believe).

00:01:32.922 --> 00:01:35.815
Two years ago most of the time went into producing.

00:01:36.115 --> 00:01:39.125
Today that part gets cheaper and deciding weighs more.

00:01:39.425 --> 00:01:43.901
Seeing it written with your own tasks avoids arguing in the abstract and shows how

00:01:43.901 --> 00:01:45.975
much of your week has already changed.

00:01:47.275 --> 00:01:48.474
Step 2.

00:01:48.774 --> 00:01:50.623
The four new competencies.

00:01:51.173 --> 00:01:56.854
Add the four new competencies: context for agents (rules, skills, specs), evals for

00:01:56.854 --> 00:02:02.603
non-deterministic outputs, reviewing AI-generated tests, and cost and infrastructure

00:02:02.603 --> 00:02:04.109
(tokens, CI, runners).

00:02:04.659 --> 00:02:09.347
Context for agents is writing rules, conventions and specifications an agent can

00:02:09.347 --> 00:02:11.047
follow without you beside it.

00:02:11.347 --> 00:02:15.895
Evals is measuring outputs that are never the same twice, with datasets and

00:02:15.895 --> 00:02:16.562
thresholds.

00:02:16.862 --> 00:02:22.044
Reviewing generated tests is spotting filler: weak assertions, duplicated cases,

00:02:22.044 --> 00:02:24.052
mocks of what should be tested.

00:02:24.352 --> 00:02:29.340
Cost and infrastructure is knowing what each run costs in tokens and CI minutes, and

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:30.647
where each thing runs.

00:02:30.947 --> 00:02:34.270
None replaces classic automation: they build on it.

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:36.652
Step 3.

00:02:36.952 --> 00:02:38.453
What doesn't change.

00:02:39.003 --> 00:02:43.893
Flag what doesn't change: risk thinking, good test design and knowing the system.

00:02:44.193 --> 00:02:46.413
That is what AI doesn't bring.

00:02:46.963 --> 00:02:51.296
Risk thinking is deciding where a failure hurts the business and focusing effort

00:02:51.296 --> 00:02:51.621
there.

00:02:51.921 --> 00:02:56.565
Good test design is knowing techniques such as partitions, boundaries and decision

00:02:56.565 --> 00:02:58.321
tables, and when to apply each.

00:02:58.621 --> 00:03:03.279
Knowing the system is understanding its dependencies, its data and its fragile

00:03:03.279 --> 00:03:03.697
points.

00:03:03.997 --> 00:03:08.284
An agent without these three produces numeric coverage, not quality.

00:03:08.584 --> 00:03:13.835
They are also the skills that let you review what the agent produces, so their value

00:03:13.835 --> 00:03:14.961
goes up, not down.

00:03:16.261 --> 00:03:17.273
Step 4.

00:03:17.573 --> 00:03:19.062
The skills matrix.

00:03:19.612 --> 00:03:25.362
Build the matrix: one row per competency, columns Level today (0 to 3), Evidence and

00:03:25.362 --> 00:03:26.047
Next step.

00:03:26.597 --> 00:03:30.825
Use one row per competency: the four new ones and the three unchanged.

00:03:31.125 --> 00:03:35.750
In the level column put a number from zero to three, where zero is never done it,

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:40.033
one is tried it, two is use it on a real project and three is can teach it.

00:03:40.333 --> 00:03:45.641
Under evidence, a link or a concrete artifact: a repo, a pipeline, a report.

00:03:45.941 --> 00:03:48.835
With no evidence, the level can't be above one.

00:03:49.135 --> 00:03:51.900
That rule is what makes the matrix honest.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:54.329
Step 5.

00:03:54.629 --> 00:03:56.884
Two focus areas, not seven.

00:03:57.434 --> 00:04:02.476
Pick two competencies at level 0 or 1 and tie each to a day of this series.

00:04:03.026 --> 00:04:05.420
Improving everything at once doesn't work.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:09.891
Pick the two lowest rows that also matter for your current job or the one you're

00:04:09.891 --> 00:04:10.204
after.

00:04:10.504 --> 00:04:15.389
Assign each a day from this series and a date: for example, evals with day eight and

00:04:15.389 --> 00:04:18.007
cost and infrastructure with day twenty-nine.

00:04:18.307 --> 00:04:23.517
The next step must be a small, visible deliverable, such as an eval harness with ten

00:04:23.517 --> 00:04:26.309
cases or a job that publishes tokens per run.

00:04:27.609 --> 00:04:28.795
Step 6.

00:04:29.095 --> 00:04:30.793
Evidence on your profile.

00:04:31.343 --> 00:04:36.665
Update your profile with concrete evidence: a repo with evals beats "experience with

00:04:36.665 --> 00:04:36.919
AI".

00:04:37.469 --> 00:04:42.561
A profile saying "experience with AI applied to testing" is indistinguishable from a

00:04:42.561 --> 00:04:43.532
thousand others.

00:04:43.832 --> 00:04:48.442
One that links a repository with an eval harness, a pipeline with a quality gate and

00:04:48.442 --> 00:04:50.034
a written triage case is not.

00:04:50.334 --> 00:04:54.909
When you finish each deliverable, add it with one sentence saying what it measures

00:04:54.909 --> 00:04:56.583
and which decision it enabled.

00:04:56.883 --> 00:05:01.287
In interviews, that evidence lets you speak from your own experience instead of

00:05:01.287 --> 00:05:04.966
repeating theory, which is what a technical interviewer looks for.

00:05:06.266 --> 00:05:07.371
An example.

00:05:07.921 --> 00:05:11.442
An SDET with six years of experience fills in the matrix.

00:05:11.742 --> 00:05:15.944
For test design and system knowledge he gives himself a three, with plenty of

00:05:15.944 --> 00:05:16.435
evidence.

00:05:16.735 --> 00:05:22.045
For context for agents, a one: he has used an assistant but never wrote rules for

00:05:22.045 --> 00:05:22.636
the repo.

00:05:22.936 --> 00:05:24.575
For evals, zero.

00:05:24.875 --> 00:05:27.386
For reviewing generated tests, one.

00:05:27.686 --> 00:05:29.337
For cost, zero.

00:05:29.637 --> 00:05:33.215
He picks evals and context for agents as focus areas.

00:05:33.515 --> 00:05:38.804
In three weeks he builds an AGENTS.md for his automation repo and a ten-case eval

00:05:38.804 --> 00:05:40.763
for the team's bug classifier.

00:05:41.063 --> 00:05:45.897
His profile goes from one generic sentence to two links with one line of explanation

00:05:45.897 --> 00:05:46.185
each.

00:05:47.485 --> 00:05:49.148
The most common mistakes.

00:05:49.698 --> 00:05:52.696
Giving yourself a high level with no artifact to back it.

00:05:53.246 --> 00:05:56.778
Picking the trendy tool as a focus instead of the competency you lack.

00:05:57.328 --> 00:06:01.139
Dropping test design and risk because "AI does that".

00:06:01.689 --> 00:06:05.198
Planning seven improvements in parallel and finishing none.

00:06:06.498 --> 00:06:08.741
To know it is done, check this.

00:06:09.291 --> 00:06:13.125
The matrix has seven rows with level, evidence and next step.

00:06:13.675 --> 00:06:16.209
No level above one lacks linked evidence.

00:06:16.759 --> 00:06:20.105
You picked two focus areas with a date and a deliverable.

00:06:20.655 --> 00:06:24.013
Your profile links at least one verifiable artifact.

00:06:25.313 --> 00:06:27.208
And where AI fell short.

00:06:27.758 --> 00:06:31.673
I asked a model to rate my level and it marked everything "advanced".

00:06:31.973 --> 00:06:35.436
It's useless for self-assessment: it flatters you.

00:06:36.736 --> 00:06:38.178
That was day 2.

00:06:38.478 --> 00:06:43.452
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:06:43.452 --> 00:06:44.460
asked questions.

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:49.621
Continue with day 3: The new ways of testing: from asserts to evals.
